Rory: heads up — users hitting a mix of build errors and runtime oddities when importing the SDK in Next middleware (Vercel Edge). stack traces mention fs/stream and our headers merge.
Priya: which version? any repro code?
Rory: starter uses redwood-js-sdk@0.8.4. Minimal middleware: ```ts
import { createClient } from 'redwood-js-sdk'
export async function middleware(req: Request) {
  const c = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
  await c.generate({ prompt: 'hi' })
  return new Response('ok')
}
``` build fails: "Can't resolve 'fs'" and runtime loses trace headers.
Samir: classic leak of node-only code into edge bundle + mutation of incoming Headers. Next's Edge bundler is aggressive. Two things we should do in SDK: (1) stop top-level imports of node builtins (fs/stream/undici) — move them to a node-only module and dynamic import under a runtime guard, (2) defensive header handling: never mutate the passed Headers instance.
Priya: agree. For (1) package.json exports should provide explicit edge/node entrypoints so bundlers can pick the right one. Example: ```json
"exports": {
  "./edge": "./dist/edge.mjs",
  "./node": "./dist/node.cjs",
  "./": { "node": "./dist/node.cjs", "default": "./dist/edge.mjs" }
}
```
Rory: good. Also watch out for top-level side effects: token-cache currently does a sync fs read at module init which kills edge builds. Move that into an async node-only function.
Samir: header util fix sketch: ```js
export function normalizeHeaders(h) {
  if (!h) return {}
  if (typeof h.entries === 'function') return Object.fromEntries(h.entries())
  if (typeof h[Symbol.iterator] === 'function') return Object.fromEntries(h)
  return {...h}
}
export function mergeHeaders(orig, extra) {
  const base = normalizeHeaders(orig)
  return typeof Headers !== 'undefined' ? new Headers({...base, ...extra}) : {...base, ...extra}
}
``` This avoids mutating orig and covers weird Headers shapes.
Priya: types are painful too — index.d.ts references Node types (Buffer, NodeJS.Timeout). Split types: dist/types/index.d.ts for DOM-friendly and dist/types/node.d.ts for node-only. Add types subpath exports and typesVersions if needed.
Rory: also need to avoid `import 'undici'` at top-level. Even a devDeps or optional dep can get bundled. Put node-only adapter in ./dist/node and dynamic import: `if (IS_NODE) { await import('./node/adapter.js') }`.
Samir: Next edge bundler scans static imports, so dynamic import is the only reliable way to keep node code out. And provide an explicit '/edge' import path in README as a workaround: `import { createClient } from 'redwood-js-sdk/edge'`.
Priya: I'll open a small PR that: - adds conditional exports - moves node-only code behind dynamic imports - adds header normalization + tests - splits types - updates starter README with middleware snippet and migration notes.
Rory: short-term snippet for users to unblock: ```ts
import { createClient } from 'redwood-js-sdk/edge'
export async function middleware(req: Request) {
  const headers = Object.fromEntries(req.headers)
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
  const out = await client.generate({ prompt: 'ping', headers })
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(out))
}
```
Samir: one more gotcha: don't initialize the client at module top-level in middleware. It can trigger sync fs/env reads in some code paths. Create inside the handler.
CI-bot: CI started for branch fix/edge-exports-types-mergeheaders (#276). running next build fixture.
Docs-bot: will draft a short "Edge migration" section for the starter and add FAQ entries: "Can't resolve 'fs'" and "Headers lost after SDK call".
Rory: thanks, please ping when canary published so we can smoke test on Vercel.
